 ## Overview
-Reparametrized Integral Gradients is a technique for differentiating rendering integrals containing visibility-related discontinuities by applying changes of variables to remove the dependence of discontinuity locations on scene parameters [S1][S2, unofficial]. Unlike explicit edge sampling methods that require detecting and sampling geometric boundaries, this method warps the integration domain so that discontinuities become static in the transformed space, allowing differentiation under the integral sign [S1][S4, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from siblings like [Reparameterized Volume Sampling](3309-reparameterized-volume-sampling.md) by focusing on path-space and spherical domain reparameterizations for general path tracing rather than just volume density sampling, and from [Boundary Path Integral Formulation](3389-boundary-path-integral-formulation.md) by avoiding the explicit decomposition into interior and boundary terms [S2, S4, unofficial].
+Reparametrized Integral Gradients is a technique for differentiable rendering that eliminates bias in gradients caused by moving visibility discontinuities by applying changes of variables to render the discontinuities static in the transformed domain [S1][S2, unofficial][S3]. Unlike explicit edge sampling methods that require detecting and sampling geometric boundaries, this method reparameterizes the integration domain (such as spherical domains for light transport) so that the discontinuity points become independent of the scene parameters $\pi$ [S2, S5, unofficial]. This allows the gradient to be computed via standard Monte Carlo integration over the interior, avoiding the boundary terms that arise when differentiating under the integral sign [S1][S5, unofficial]. It is distinct from [Reparameterized Volume Sampling](3309-reparameterized-volume-sampling.md) which focuses on density fields, and [Boundary Path Integral Formulation](3389-boundary-path-integral-formulation.md) which explicitly separates and samples boundary terms [S2, unofficial][S3].
 
 ## How it works
-The method addresses the bias introduced when scene parameters $\pi$ (e.g., object position, shape) affect visibility, causing discontinuities in the integrand to move as $\pi$ changes [S3]. Naïve differentiation yields incorrect gradients because the moving boundary contributes a term that is ignored [S3]. The core mechanism is to transform the integral such that the discontinuity location becomes independent of $\pi$ [S2, unofficial].
+The method addresses the problem where the rendering integral $I(\pi)$ contains a discontinuity at a point $T(\pi)$ that depends on a parameter $\pi$ (e.g., object position or shape). Direct differentiation yields a boundary term that is difficult to estimate [S3]. The method transforms the integral into a domain where the discontinuity is stationary.
 
